# 3. DUAL-HAND GESTURE WEAPON SELECTION

Modify the existing weapon wheel interaction.

The weapon wheel must now use BOTH HANDS simultaneously.

IMPORTANT CONTROL RULE:

- LEFT HAND = OPEN AND CLOSE THE WEAPON WHEEL.
- RIGHT HAND = SELECT THE WEAPON.

Do not reverse these responsibilities.

### 3.1 Left Hand — Weapon Wheel Activation

The left hand controls the weapon wheel state.

**Closed Fist = OPEN WEAPON WHEEL**

When the player makes a stable fist with the left hand:
- Open the semicircular weapon wheel.
- Activate the existing slow-motion effect.
- Apply background blur.
- Display all weapon options.
- Enable right-hand weapon selection.

**Open Hand = CLOSE WEAPON WHEEL**

When the player opens or unclenches their left hand:
- Close the weapon wheel.
- Remove background blur.
- Restore normal gameplay speed.
- Disable weapon selection through the right hand.

This must work as a HOLD-BASED interaction.

The weapon wheel remains open as long as the left hand is detected as a closed fist.

The weapon wheel must NOT automatically close after selecting a weapon.

The player can continue changing weapons while keeping the left hand clenched.

### 3.2 Right Hand — Weapon Selection

When the weapon wheel is open, recognize the number of extended fingers on the RIGHT HAND.

Use the following mapping:

| Right-hand gesture | Weapon |
|---|---|
| 1 finger | Assault Rifle (AR) |
| 2 fingers | SMG |
| 3 fingers | Flamethrower |
| 4 fingers | Grenade |
| 5 fingers | No selection |

The player must be able to switch between weapons by changing the right-hand finger count while maintaining the left-hand fist.

The weapon wheel stays open until the left hand is opened.

### 3.3 Selection Behavior

When a valid gesture is recognized:

- Highlight the corresponding weapon slot.
- Update the currently selected weapon.
- Play a subtle selection sound.
- Update ammunition information.
- Update the weapon HUD.
- Keep the weapon wheel visible.

Do not automatically close the weapon wheel after selection.

If the same finger count remains detected, do not repeatedly trigger weapon selection or spam selection audio.

Allow a new selection when the right-hand gesture changes to another valid finger count.

### 3.4 Hand Tracking Architecture

The system must track both hands simultaneously.

Requirements:
- Correct left/right hand identification.
- Independent landmark processing for each hand.
- Correct handling of mirrored webcam input.
- Finger-count detection for the right hand.
- Fist and open-hand detection for the left hand.
- Landmark smoothing.
- Gesture stability thresholds.
- Confidence thresholds.
- Protection against accidental selections.

Suggested initial detection settings:
- Gesture activation stability: 200-300 ms.
- Weapon selection stability: 200-300 ms.
- Configurable tracking-loss timeout.

If the right hand temporarily disappears, keep the current selected weapon without making a random selection.

If the left hand temporarily loses tracking, briefly preserve its previous state to avoid flickering.

If the left hand remains undetected beyond a configurable safety timeout, close the weapon wheel and restore normal gameplay.

The webcam tracking overlay must remain visible and responsive.

### 3.5 Important Interaction Rules

- Right-hand selection is disabled when the weapon wheel is closed.
- Left-hand fist does not select weapons.
- Right-hand fist does not control the weapon wheel.
- Opening the left hand always closes the wheel.
- Five extended fingers on the right hand must not close the wheel.
- Weapon selection must not trigger multiple times from one gesture.
- Keep existing aiming and firing controls functional.
- Do not allow gesture detection to interfere with shooting.

Provide keyboard fallback controls for testing and accessibility.

